-- Bebop.Conformance -- F4 conformance harness (v2)
-- Replaces the scaffold's Conformance.lean.
--
-- This module:
--  (a) Declares the EXPECT rows for 80 positive + 14 negative constructs
--      as a Lean structure. COUNTED 2026-09-14: those are 80 and 14, not the
--      "75 + 11" this line used to claim, and the SUITE is bigger than both --
--      `ls bench/parity_constructs/*.bp | wc -l` = 101 and
--      `ls bench/parity_constructs/neg/*.bp | wc -l` = 20, all 121 of them
--      carrying a `// EXPECT <v> from:` header. So the ROADMAP F4 denominator
--      `lean_conformance: c/86` is stale too: the real denominator is 121, of
--      which this file declares 94 rows.
--  (b) Declares 97 program entries with their VERBATIM .bp source (see the
--      note on `oracleEntries`; 88 of those sources used to be invented).
--  (c) Defines runConstruct : Program → Result and checkAgainst : Result → Expected → Bool.
--  (d) Provides #eval-ground sample results for the 10 constructs that the
--      interpreter can run WITHOUT a parser (inline AST construction), each
--      with a `#guard` so `lake build` fails if one regresses:
--        c01_lit c02_arith c07_while c08_call c13_array
--        c11_enum c12_match c22_matchbind c25_matchtail c96_enumpay
--      That is the honest conformance number for this row: 10 of 121.
--      The other 111 are DATA here, not executed: there is no .bp parser in
--      `formal/`, so every remaining construct needs its AST hand-encoded.
--      Writing that parser is the single highest-leverage F4 task left.
--
-- Off-box note: the full harness is #eval-driven by harness.lean;
-- this file provides the data and the check logic.

